.TH WINDOW 1
.SH NAME
window, terminal \- run a command in a window
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B window
[
.I cmd
[
.I arg ...
]
]
.PP
.B terminal
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Window
opens a window on the draw server and runs
.I cmd
in it, with the window as the command's three descriptors.
With no
.IR cmd ,
it runs
.BR /bin/rc .
A name with no
.B /
is resolved against
.B /bin
only, not
.BR $path ,
which is what a person types at a prompt.
.PP
.I Terminal
is the same program with the command fixed to
.BR /bin/rc ,
built as its own binary rather than run as
.BR "window /bin/rc" .
.IR init (8)
starts one at boot for the desktop's own shell.
.PP
Either one mounts
.B /srv/draw
at
.BR /mnt ,
claims a new window there, and binds that window's directory over
.B /dev
so that
.B /dev/cons
is the window's keyboard and
.B /dev/consctl
its raw switch (see
.IR intuition (4)).
The command runs with a copy of the descriptor table, its standard
input and output and error replaced by two pipes, and
.I window
or
.I terminal
then does two things at once: it reads the command's output and draws
it into the window as a grid of fixed-width cells, and it reads the
window's keyboard, edits a line at the cursor, and hands each finished
line to the command's input.
An interrupt typed in the window
.RI ( ^C )
is posted as
.B interrupt
to the command's own process group, not to
.I window
or
.IR terminal .
When the command's output pipe closes, the window closes with it.
.PP
The client area is as many eight-pixel columns and sixteen-pixel rows
as the window's geometry allows, up to ninety-six columns and
thirty-two rows.
A newline, a return, a backspace and a tab do what a terminal expects;
a line past the last row scrolls the rest up.
.SH FILES
.B /srv/draw
.br
.B /mnt/new
.br
.BI /mnt/ n /data
.br
.BI /mnt/ n /ctl
.br
.BI /mnt/ n /cons
.br
.BI /mnt/ n /consctl
.SH SOURCE
.B cmd/window/main.odin
.br
.B apps/terminal/main.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR rc (1),
.IR muidemo (1),
.IR intuition (4),
.IR draw (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Before the command starts,
.I window
and
.I terminal
end with a number rather than a word: 0x74 if
.B /srv/draw
would not mount or a new window would not open, 0x75 if a window file
would not open, 0x76 for a geometry too small to draw on, 0x78 if a
draw write was refused or the window's own console would not open, and
0x72 if the command itself would not start.
Once the command is running, the exit status is empty, or
.B ioproc
or
.B threadcreate
if the internal machinery that serves both descriptors at once could
not be set up, or
.B read
if the keyboard ended first.
.SH BUGS
The window's title is always set to
.BR terminal ,
even when
.I window
runs a command other than
.BR rc .
Standard input cannot be given to
.I window
from a pipe; it is always the new window's keyboard.
